CAT(1)                     Funny CLI Manual                     CAT(1)

NAME
    cat — Display the contents of a file, because opening an editor
    would be far too much work.

SYNOPSIS
    cat <file>

DESCRIPTION
    The cat command reads a file and displays its contents directly in
    the terminal.

    The file is opened as text using UTF-8 encoding. Characters that
    cannot be decoded are replaced instead of causing the command to
    fail.

USAGE
    > cat <file>       # Displays the contents of the specified file.

FUN FACTS
    - cat does not actually summon a feline.
    - The name comes from "concatenate", even though most people use it just to read files.
    - cat is one of the fastest ways to discover what is inside a file.
    - If you cat a very large file, your terminal may regret your decision.

EXAMPLES
    > cat notes.txt
        Displays the contents of notes.txt.

    > cat /home/user/config.txt
        Displays the contents of config.txt using an absolute path.

    > cat missing.txt
        Displays an error because the requested file does not exist.

SEE ALSO
    help(1), ls(1), cd(1), man(1)

    Remember, cat is basically opening a file,
    but with considerably less clicking.

